WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:06.580
Let's go next to our caller from the 6-3,
one area code who's calling today from 6 3

00:00:06.580 --> 00:00:07.580
1.

00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:08.710
David is.

00:00:08.710 --> 00:00:10.839
Yes, it is.

00:00:10.839 --> 00:00:11.839
Hi.

00:00:11.839 --> 00:00:15.209
This is spinning chair from Long Island.

00:00:15.209 --> 00:00:16.480
Thanks, Bernie.

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:20.390
Before you go on, if you can put the phone
a little further from your mouth because you're

00:00:20.390 --> 00:00:23.840
absolutely maxing out our system and it becomes
hard to hear.

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:26.820
Just keep the phone a little further away
from your mouth, please.

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:28.440
And text right from here the other day.

00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:29.440
I love it.

00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:30.440
Yes.

00:00:30.440 --> 00:00:35.219
The other day, you had a video about social
democracy and populism that I found very interesting.

00:00:35.219 --> 00:00:41.000
But I thought there was more to flush out
because you yourself do have an anti-establishment

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:42.000
criticism.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:46.460
You know, you will criticize what you call
the Democratic establishment.

00:00:46.460 --> 00:00:50.210
And I think that to an extent is populism.

00:00:50.210 --> 00:00:54.559
So I don't know if you can kind of square
that circle because you do engage in some

00:00:54.559 --> 00:01:01.480
populist arguments, but you do manage not
to, I guess, fall off the deep end with it.

00:01:01.480 --> 00:01:03.839
I mean, I guess I don't.

00:01:03.839 --> 00:01:05.840
There's really no conflict between.

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:12.450
So the analysis was basically an analysis
of it's harder to pervert and bamboozle with

00:01:12.450 --> 00:01:14.750
social democracy than with populism.

00:01:14.750 --> 00:01:17.000
That was my assertion, essentially.

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:22.560
And as I said in the video, that doesn't mean
left populist ideas are wrong.

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:29.690
So the way that I square it is I believe in
social, socially democratic policy.

00:01:29.690 --> 00:01:36.149
And certainly there are good left populist
ideas and I have some of those ideas.

00:01:36.149 --> 00:01:42.789
But the analysis was that it's very easy to
pervert populism and you end up with right

00:01:42.789 --> 00:01:45.939
populism and it can even go into authoritarianism.

00:01:45.939 --> 00:01:47.520
That was the analysis I made.

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:49.270
Maybe I'm not understanding the question,
but I don't know.

00:01:49.270 --> 00:01:50.350
There's anything to square.

00:01:50.350 --> 00:01:56.460
Oh, so in other words, you wouldn't say that
you have a somewhat populist analysis, but

00:01:56.460 --> 00:01:59.560
that you don't go off the edge with that.

00:01:59.560 --> 00:02:05.369
Like maybe a moderate populism, you would
just say that you're just a social Democrat.

00:02:05.369 --> 00:02:10.209
Listen, the word getting into is sort of like
labeling everything.

00:02:10.209 --> 00:02:17.069
I guess my general political views, economic
views are of of social democracy.

00:02:17.069 --> 00:02:22.280
There is certainly overlap in terms of many
of the goals with left populism.

00:02:22.280 --> 00:02:27.950
But the story was not so much about my views
per say, but about the ways in which populist

00:02:27.950 --> 00:02:32.470
rhetoric has been perverted in many cases
and ends up being something very different

00:02:32.470 --> 00:02:34.769
than what's promised.

00:02:34.769 --> 00:02:41.750
OK, well, maybe if I could phrase it in a
different way.

00:02:41.750 --> 00:02:43.599
I just lost my train of thought.

00:02:43.599 --> 00:02:44.840
I'll head out now.

00:02:44.840 --> 00:02:45.840
Have a good day, David.

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:46.840
All right.

00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:47.840
Very good.

00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:48.840
Appreciate the phone call.

00:02:48.840 --> 00:02:49.840
Great to hear from you.

00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:50.840
Yeah.

00:02:50.840 --> 00:02:51.840
People, so we have dramatically improved our
phone system over the last two weeks where

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:54.720
we have a very small delay, as people can
probably tell.

00:02:54.720 --> 00:03:01.150
The only downside is I have less of an ability
when that crunching sound happens, it's it's

00:03:01.150 --> 00:03:02.209
in the system.

00:03:02.209 --> 00:03:03.410
It's not on my end.

00:03:03.410 --> 00:03:07.250
So people need to talk a little quieter and
maybe keep the phone a little further away.

00:03:07.250 --> 00:03:10.680
Both very, very important elements of making
a good phone call right now

